{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Yenisoy2019-Ge-MWIR-AR3",
  "Source": "A. Yenisoy, C. Yesilyaprak, K. Ruzgar, and S. Tuzemen, \"Ultra-broad band antireflection coating at mid wave infrared for high efficient germanium optics,\" Opt. Mater. Express 9, 3123-3131 (2019). doi:10.1364/OME.9.003123 - Table 1 (p. 3127): layer thicknesses 135 / 105 / 725 nm, materials nL / nH / nL; materials Al2O3 (L) and Ge (H) from the abstract and Sec. 4; dispersion from Eq. (3) with constants in Sec. 4 (pp. 3125-3126)",
  "Notes": "Ultra-broadband MWIR antireflection coating on germanium (3 layers, under 1 um total), 3.4-4.8 um\nSpecification: Minimise reflectance (maximise transmittance) of each coated Ge surface over 3.4-4.8 um at normal incidence, unpolarised; 3 % thickness tolerance per layer (Table 2).\nReported: Total physical thickness 965 nm (p. 3127; 135+105+725 = 965, matches). Simulated (Table 3, p. 3128): Rav 0.253 %, Tav 99.747 % over 3.4-4.8 um; measured Rav 0.256 %, Tav 99.744 % (measured at AOI 10 deg). Measured: base R 0.005 % and peak T 99.995 % at 3550 nm; R < 0.5 % and T > 99.5 % everywhere from 3.4 to 4.8 um (p. 3128). Fig. 8 (single-surface R, %): measured curve min ~0.005 at ~3550 nm, max ~0.45 at ~4130 nm, ~0.11 at 4800 nm, ~0.28 at 3400 nm; the 'simulated' curve (red, likely for the as-built thicknesses) min ~0.05 at ~3590 nm, max ~0.44 at ~4170 nm, ~0.21 at 4800 nm. Check with these coefficients (this extraction): R min 0.021 % at 3570 nm, R max 0.41 % at 4130 nm, average R 0.24 % over 3400-4800 nm, R(3400) 0.35 %, R(4800) 0.12 % - shape matches Fig. 8.\nNotes: Table 1 numbers layers from the SUBSTRATE (OptiLayer convention): layer 1 = 135 nm nL on Ge, layer 3 = 725 nm nL facing air. Reversed here so layer 1 faces air. Confirmed by recomputation: the reversed order gives the Fig. 8 AR curve, the Table order read from air gives R ~ 70 %. The QWOT column of Table 1 (1.6 / 3.9 / 8.6) is stated at a 550 nm monitor wavelength with visible indices (n ~ 1.63 for Al2O3, ~5.1 for Ge) that the paper does not give, so the nm thicknesses are used. The Ge film uses the Ge substrate Sellmeier (Sec. 4: 'Resulting refractive index dispersion ... for Ge was also used for Ge film'). Both sides of the 1 mm Ge plate were coated identically; reported R/T values (R + T ~ 100 %) are effectively per-surface values with absorption neglected, so compare single-surface R. Unpolarised, AOI 0 for design; measurement at 10 deg. reference_nm 4000 is a placeholder (not given).\nLayers from Table 1 (p. 3127): layer thicknesses 135 / 105 / 725 nm, materials nL / nH / nL; materials Al2O3 (L) and Ge (H) from the abstract and Sec. 4; dispersion from Eq. (3) with constants in Sec. 4 (pp. 3125-3126); layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours Rav 0.238 %, R max 0.41 % over 3.4-4.8 um; paper Rav 0.253 % (simulated), R < 0.5 % throughout.",
